<section class="hero"><div class="wrap"><div class="eyebrow">Trust-Worthy AI · Truth Trial 000004</div><h1>Who is Jesus?</h1><p>Not “what do churches say about him?” The question is what the earliest available evidence can establish, where history stops, and where people make a theological judgment.</p><div class="manifesto"><strong>Question under test:</strong> Was Jesus merely a teacher, a failed messianic claimant, an apocalyptic prophet, or the unique revelation of God claimed by the earliest Christians?</div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="wrap grid"><article class="card"><div class="case-head"><div><div class="case-id"><?php echo $caseId; ?></div><h2>Provisional finding</h2></div><div class="status">OPEN TO CHALLENGE</div></div><div class="finding"><strong>What survives the first pass:</strong> Jesus of Nazareth was a real first-century Jewish teacher, was crucified under Pontius Pilate, and very early followers proclaimed that God raised him and made him Lord. The historical evidence does not make “Jesus never existed” the best explanation. It also does not allow a historian to compel one final conclusion about his divinity; that conclusion rests on how one weighs his teachings, actions, resurrection claims, and worldview.</div>
<h3>Earliest layer: the letters</h3><p>Paul's undisputed letters are the earliest surviving Christian texts. They present Jesus as crucified, risen, Lord, and central to God's saving action—not as a later legend invented centuries afterward. First Corinthians 15 preserves a tradition Paul says he received and passed on, including appearances claimed by Peter, the Twelve, James, and others. The date and exact form of the tradition remain debated, but it is early enough that the resurrection proclamation cannot honestly be dismissed as a late medieval invention.</p>
<h3>Gospel traditions: historical source and theological witness</h3><p>The Gospels were written to proclaim faith, not to function as modern neutral biographies. That matters. Yet their theological purpose does not automatically erase historical memory. Across the traditions, Jesus announces God's kingdom, gathers disciples, teaches in memorable forms, clashes with authorities, is executed, and is reported alive afterward. The differences between accounts must be faced rather than hidden; agreement alone is not proof, but neither are differences proof that nothing historical remains.</p>
<h3>Outside the New Testament</h3><p>Josephus refers to Jesus and to James “the brother of Jesus who was called Christ,” though one Josephan paragraph has later Christian editing and must be handled with caution. Tacitus reports that Christus was executed under Pontius Pilate during Tiberius' reign and that the movement continued. These sources do not establish miracles. They do matter because they make a purely invented founder less plausible.</p>
<h3>What Jesus claimed</h3><p>In the surviving records, Jesus does not fit comfortably into the role of a harmless moral teacher. He announces the reign of God, speaks and acts with unusual authority, forgives sins, calls for allegiance above family and possessions, receives titles his followers take in extraordinary directions, and makes his own person inseparable from his message. Whether these traditions preserve his exact self-understanding is debated. But the “just a nice teacher” reduction leaves major portions of the evidence unexplained.</p>
<h3>The resurrection question</h3><p>History can establish that followers sincerely claimed encounters with the risen Jesus and that this conviction transformed and spread the movement. It cannot run a repeatable experiment on a miracle. Competing explanations include visionary experience, legend development, deliberate invention, unknown natural events, and actual resurrection. Each has costs. Trust-Worthy will not call the resurrection “proven” merely because it is believed, nor will it call it impossible merely because it is extraordinary.</p>
<div class="labels"><span class="label">Strong: Jesus existed</span><span class="label">Strong: crucifixion</span><span class="label">Strong: early resurrection proclamation</span><span class="label">Disputed: source precision</span><span class="label">Disputed: divine identity</span></div><p class="muted">The case remains open. The honest question is not whether people have opinions about Jesus; it is which explanation best accounts for the earliest evidence without deleting the parts we dislike.</p></article>
<aside class="card"><h3>Evidence map</h3><ul class="rules"><li>Paul: 1 Corinthians 15; Galatians 1–2; Philippians 2.</li><li>Gospel accounts: Mark, Matthew, Luke, John.</li><li>Josephus, Antiquities 18 and 20—textual caution required.</li><li>Tacitus, Annals 15.44.</li><li>Early creedal and resurrection traditions.</li><li>Arguments for and against miracle claims.</li></ul><h3 style="margin-top:28px">Primary-source starting points</h3><ul class="rules"><li><a href="https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0132:book=18:section=3">Josephus, Antiquities 18</a></li><li><a href="https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Tac.+Ann.+15.44">Tacitus, Annals 15.44</a></li><li>1 Corinthians 15; Galatians 1–2.</li></ul><div class="finding"><strong>Current classification:</strong><br>Historically real, historically crucified, early proclaimed as risen. Ultimate identity remains the central disputed judgment.</div></aside></div></section>
